      "id": 1,
      "text": "Anatone was named after a Nez Perce woman who lived in the area, a detail noted by both Wikipedia and local historians.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anatone%2C_Washington",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s first settlers, Daniel McIvor and Charles Isecke, arrived in 1878 and established the community that still thrives today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anatone%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Anatone sits just south of the Idaho–Oregon–Washington tripoint, making it a quiet gateway to three states.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anatone%2C_Washington",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The local park spans 800 acres, offering year‑round activities from hiking to birdwatching in a truly rustic setting.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.islands.com/2071287/anatone-washington-tiny-roadside-community-rustic-gem-gorgeous-views-blue-mountains-outdoor-fun/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A moderate 2.7‑mile hike to the park’s summit rewards hikers with panoramic views of the Wallowa Mountains, Hells Canyon, and the Grande Ronde River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
